<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Banate of Macsó</h1><p class="st-meta">1254 CE – 1496 CE (approx.) · capital Macsó</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hungarian banate south of the Sava around Mačva and Belgrade, a frontier province contested with Serbia for over two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1254 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary">Béla IV of Hungary</a> created a banate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macva">Mačva</a> region south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and gave it to his son-in-law, the Rus prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostislav-mikhailovich">Rostislav Mikhailovich</a>, who also held Bosnian lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The ban governed a mixed Serbian and Hungarian population from Macsó and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, guarding the southern frontier and the trade route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. From 1284 it passed to the deposed Serbian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dragutin">Stefan Dragutin</a> as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-syrmia">Kingdom of Syrmia</a>. Later bans were Hungarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, notably the Garai family in the 14th and 15th centuries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the Ottoman advance, the banate lost its strategic role; the office lapsed in the late 15th century, and the region fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> itself taken in 1521.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banate-of-macso.jpg" alt="Banate of Macsó" width="320" height="379"><figcaption>Image: WikiEditor2004 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macvanska_banovina.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1254 CE – 1496 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Macsó</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rostislav Mikhailovich, Stefan Dragutin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity; Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian; Serbian; Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banate of Mačva, Duchy of Macsó</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1375&amp;focus=banate-of-macso" aria-label="Banate of Macsó on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5174 617 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banate of Macsó lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0M5475 856h0M5446 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5494 873h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5494 873h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Serbia. <a href="/atlas?year=1375&amp;focus=banate-of-macso">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

